# =============================================================================
# not-uber-service — MASTER SETTINGS FILE
# =============================================================================
# Copy to .env and edit:   make init   (or: cp .env.example .env)
#
# This one file configures the whole stack. The root docker-compose.yaml
# includes every component without an `env_file:`, so all fourteen of them
# resolve their ${...} from here. The per-component .env.example files stay
# as the reference for what each component consumes, and are what a
# standalone single-component run uses; they play no part in the full stack.
#
# THE ONLY THING YOU MUST EDIT IS SECTION 1. Everything below it has a
# working default and can be left alone on a first deployment.
#
# Values may reference values defined ABOVE them (${LIKE_THIS}), which is how
# the three names PostgreSQL's password goes by stay one secret.
#
# `make preflight` refuses to deploy while any change-me placeholder is left.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# 1. SECRETS — CHANGE EVERY ONE OF THESE
# =============================================================================
# Nothing here has a safe default on purpose: the compose files use
# ${VAR:?...} so a missing value fails immediately instead of starting a
# cluster with a password somebody could guess.

# PostgreSQL superuser. Created by Patroni on first bootstrap, then used by
# bootstrap, the generators, dispatch, city-service and Debezium.
PG_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=change-me
# Replication between the Patroni nodes. Never used by an application.
PG_REPLICATION_PASSWORD=change-me-too
# Guards Patroni's REST API: switchover, failover, restart, reload. The
# read-only endpoints stay open, because the HAProxy checks depend on them.
PATRONI_REST_PASSWORD=change-me-rest

# Redis. One password for the data nodes, used by every service.
REDIS_PASSWORD=change-me-redis

# ClickHouse. Created by the image on first start; Grafana, Superset,
# bootstrap and the sink all log in with it.
CH_PASSWORD=change-me-clickhouse

# ksqlDB's HTTP Basic auth. Kafka itself has no authentication anywhere, so
# this is the one place in the streaming layer that actually gates access —
# DBeaver's ksqlDB driver requires a login regardless. Must not contain ':'
# or ',': the credential is stored one-per-line as `user: password,role` and
# either character would be ambiguous to parse back out.
KSQLDB_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-ksqldb

# Grafana admin login.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-grafana

# Superset admin login, and the key that signs its session cookies.
# Losing SUPERSET_SECRET_KEY means saved database passwords can no longer be
# decrypted, so keep it with the rest of these.
SUPERSET_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-superset
SUPERSET_SECRET_KEY=change-me-superset-secret

# ---- derived from the above; do not edit ------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL's password reaches its three consumers under three different
# names. They must be the same secret, so they are written once above and
# referenced here rather than repeated.
PG_PASSWORD=${PG_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD}
CDC_PG_PASSWORD=${PG_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD}


# =============================================================================
# 2. STACK-WIDE
# =============================================================================
# Every container runs UTC, and every stored timestamp is UTC.
TZ=UTC

# Reserved, and NOT yet wired up: no component reads this today. The demand
# curve lives as a fixed hour-by-hour table in the generators and is indexed
# by the UTC hour, so the busy hours currently fall at UTC rush hour rather
# than New York's. Set this when the pacing config it belongs to exists.
SIM_TIMEZONE=America/New_York

# How much the Python components say. DEBUG on the whole stack is a lot;
# prefer restarting one container with it.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO


# Which network the IMAGE BUILDS use. Defaults to the host's own network: a
# build stage's traffic is forwarded through the bridge, which is exactly
# what a route in front of this host often cannot see, even while the
# host's own connections work fine. The give-away, if this were the wrong
# setting: `docker pull` works (the daemon is a host process) while the
# first `apt-get` inside a build is refused.
#
# It has no effect on how the stack runs: every container still joins the
# nus-backbone bridge either way, regardless of this value.
#
# Set to `default` if this host has no such gap — a plain deployment with
# nothing in front of it, for instance.
BUILD_NETWORK=host


# =============================================================================
# 3. WHERE THE DATA LIVES
# =============================================================================
# All 29 named volumes are bound to a directory under this root, instead of
# being left in Docker's own volume area. That keeps the databases, the
# topics, the warehouse and the downloaded street map on whichever disk you
# want them on, without touching the Docker daemon's configuration.
#
# Point it at the largest, fastest disk you have. Images stay wherever the
# Docker daemon's data-root is; only the data comes here. That split is
# deliberate: the images are ~15 GB and static, whereas ClickHouse grows
# 1-2 GB a day and PostgreSQL and Kafka want the faster spindle.
#
# `make init` creates the tree. `make preflight` refuses to deploy if the
# root is missing or not writable.
#
# ONE THING TO KNOW: because these are bind mounts, `docker volume rm` and
# `docker compose down -v` remove the volume ENTRY but leave the files on
# disk. `make destroy` and `make clean` therefore delete this tree
# explicitly — nothing else does, and a stale tree would be found by the
# next bring-up as if it were fresh.
NUS_VOLUME_ROOT=/data-root/lssd/nus


# =============================================================================
# 4. HOST PORTS (lb-a / lb-b entry tier)
# =============================================================================
# Component containers publish nothing. Everything enters through the pair.
# lb-b is the failover twin and offsets every port by +10000, because a host
# cannot bind the same port twice. Change these if something on the host
# already holds one — `make preflight` tells you which.

LB_A_PG_WRITE_PORT=5432
LB_A_PG_READ_PORT=5433
LB_A_REDIS_WRITE_PORT=6379
LB_A_REDIS_READ_PORT=6380
LB_A_KSQLDB_PORT=8089
LB_A_DEBEZIUM_PORT=8083
LB_A_CH_HTTP_PORT=8123
LB_A_CH_NATIVE_PORT=9000
LB_A_GRAFANA_PORT=3000
LB_A_SUPERSET_PORT=8088
LB_A_STATS_PORT=8404

LB_B_PG_WRITE_PORT=15432
LB_B_PG_READ_PORT=15433
LB_B_REDIS_WRITE_PORT=16379
LB_B_REDIS_READ_PORT=16380
LB_B_KSQLDB_PORT=18089
LB_B_DEBEZIUM_PORT=18083
LB_B_CH_HTTP_PORT=18123
LB_B_CH_NATIVE_PORT=19000
LB_B_GRAFANA_PORT=13000
LB_B_SUPERSET_PORT=18088
LB_B_STATS_PORT=18404

# Kafka's own six ports (9094-9099, one per broker per advertised address)
# are NOT listed here: they are fixed literals in the root docker-compose.yaml,
# not a variable, and lb-a only — see the comment next to them there.


# =============================================================================
# 5. IMAGES (all pinned to an exact version)
# =============================================================================
# `make pull` fetches every one of these and is the first thing to run on a
# new host: a tag that has moved or been withdrawn should surface then, not
# halfway through a bring-up.
#
# Every tag here names an exact release. A floating tag like `redis:8.2` or
# `python:3.13-slim` would mean two builds months apart quietly produce
# different images, which is the opposite of what this repository is for.

# The version stamped on the eleven images this repository builds. It is the
# STACK's version, not any base image's — which base each one was built on is
# recorded in its org.opencontainers.image.base.name label, so:
#   docker image inspect nus/superset:0.1.0 \
#     -f '{{index .Config.Labels "org.opencontainers.image.base.name"}}'
NUS_VERSION=0.1.0

# Entry tier.
HAPROXY_IMAGE=haproxy:3.4.4

# a-infra-postgres. PostGIS and pgRouting are built on top of this base.
PG_IMAGE=postgres:18.6
ETCD_IMAGE=quay.io/coreos/etcd:v3.6.14
OPENSSL_IMAGE=alpine/openssl:3.5.8

# b-infra-redis.
REDIS_IMAGE=redis:8.10.1

# c-infra-kafka. The Schema Registry, ksqlDB and the Avro converter Debezium
# uses are all Confluent components and must stay on the same major line.
KAFKA_IMAGE=apache/kafka:4.3.1
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_IMAGE=confluentinc/cp-schema-registry:8.3.1
KSQLDB_IMAGE=confluentinc/cp-ksqldb-server:8.3.1
AVRO_CONVERTER_VERSION=8.3.1
BUSYBOX_IMAGE=busybox:1.38.0

# d-infra-debezium.
CONNECT_IMAGE=quay.io/debezium/connect:3.6.2.Final

# e-infra-clickhouse. 26.8 is the current Long-Term Support line —
# ClickHouse marks one in March and one in August each year and supports it
# for twelve months, so this one runs to August 2027. Server and Keeper must
# always be the same version.
CH_SERVER_IMAGE=clickhouse/clickhouse-server:26.8.2.7
CH_KEEPER_IMAGE=clickhouse/clickhouse-keeper:26.8.2.7

# f-infra-grafana. The plugin is baked into the image at build time, so
# Grafana never reaches the internet at start-up.
GRAFANA_IMAGE=grafana/grafana:12.4.10
GRAFANA_CLICKHOUSE_PLUGIN_VERSION=4.21.2

# g-infra-superset. The ClickHouse driver is added to Superset's own
# virtualenv at build time — see g-infra-superset/Dockerfile for why that
# distinction matters.
SUPERSET_IMAGE=apache/superset:6.1.0
CLICKHOUSE_CONNECT_VERSION=1.7.2


# =============================================================================
# 6. a-infra-postgres — the OLTP cluster
# =============================================================================
# The Patroni cluster name and the etcd cluster it elects its leader in.
# Non-secret etcd settings live in a-infra-postgres/etcd.env (committed).
PATRONI_SCOPE=nus-pg
PATRONI_REST_USER=patroni

# How everything reaches PostgreSQL: through the proxy, never a pg-* node by
# name. The leader is elected and moves; only the proxies know where it is.
PG_HOST=nus-lb-a
PG_WRITE_PORT=5432
# PG_READ_PORT is the replica pool. Superset registers a second, read-only
# connection here so SQL Lab can explore the OLTP schema; an exploratory query
# typed in a browser has no business on the node the platform writes to.
PG_READ_PORT=5433
PG_DATABASE=nus
PG_USER=postgres


# =============================================================================
# 7. b-infra-redis — the cache
# =============================================================================
# The primary is elected by Sentinel. REDIS_MASTER_HOST is only the seed
# primary of a cold start; after the first failover the nodes' own rewritten
# config decides, and changing this has no further effect.
REDIS_MASTER_NAME=nus-cache
REDIS_MASTER_HOST=nus-redis-1
# Below the 4 GB container limit on purpose: an AOF rewrite forks, and with
# no swap there is nothing to absorb the spike.
REDIS_MAXMEMORY=2560mb
SENTINEL_QUORUM=2
SENTINEL_DOWN_AFTER_MS=5000
SENTINEL_FAILOVER_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# How the services find Redis: they ask Sentinel who is primary right now.
REDIS_SENTINELS=nus-sentinel-1:26379,nus-sentinel-2:26379,nus-sentinel-3:26379


# =============================================================================
# 8. c-infra-kafka — the streaming backbone
# =============================================================================
# Generated once and then never changed: every broker in one cluster must
# share this id, and changing it on a cluster that holds data orphans it.
KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID=2DgQxcGwSsWn1QTQzKeEFg
# The user inside the Kafka image, which the one-shot hands the volumes to.
# Confirm with:  docker run --rm apache/kafka:4.0.0 id
KAFKA_UID=1000
KAFKA_GID=1000
KAFKA_RETENTION_HOURS=48

# What an external client is told to dial for each broker. Unlike every
# other proxied port, this can't fall back to "whichever host address you
# used" — a broker's advertised address is fixed in its own config at
# startup and embedded in every metadata response, so it has to be a real
# answer, not a wildcard. Two slots because a host with more than one real
# network path needs a client on either one to work; `make init` fills
# these in from this host's own interfaces the same way `make urls` lists
# them, so the common case needs no manual edit — override either line if
# the guess is wrong for how you actually reach this host.
KAFKA_ADVERTISED_HOST_A=change-me-kafka-host-a
KAFKA_ADVERTISED_HOST_B=change-me-kafka-host-b

KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP=nus-kafka-1:9092,nus-kafka-2:9092,nus-kafka-3:9092
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_URL=http://nus-schema-registry:8081
# The login DBeaver (or anything else) uses against ksqlDB. The password
# half is a real secret — see KSQLDB_ADMIN_PASSWORD in section 1.
KSQLDB_ADMIN_USER=admin


# =============================================================================
# 9. d-infra-debezium — change data capture
# =============================================================================
# Debezium takes a single hostname, so it cannot fail over from nus-lb-a to
# nus-lb-b by itself. Documented in d-infra-debezium/README.md.
CDC_PG_HOST=nus-lb-a
CDC_PG_PORT=5432
CDC_PG_DATABASE=nus
CDC_PG_USER=postgres


# =============================================================================
# 10. e-infra-clickhouse — the analytics database
# =============================================================================
CH_USER=nus
CH_DATABASE=nus
# Through the entry tier, so queries and inserts spread over healthy nodes.
CH_HOST=nus-lb-a
CH_HTTP_PORT=8123


# =============================================================================
# 11. f-infra-grafana / g-infra-superset — the dashboards
# =============================================================================
GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
SUPERSET_ADMIN_USER=admin
SUPERSET_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.invalid


# =============================================================================
# 12. THE SIMULATED CITY
# =============================================================================
# Nothing to set here: the city's zones are NYC TLC's own real Taxi Zones
# (TLC_ZONES_URL, section on the map, above), restored into city_zones by
# make lion-prepare/bootstrap the same way the routable graph is. bootstrap
# and every service that places anything on the map load that same table
# (nus_common.citygrid.CityGrid), so they always draw the same real city -
# there is no grid size or bounding box left to configure.


# =============================================================================
# 13. h-bootstrap — what gets created, once
# =============================================================================
# How much h-bootstrap may use. The routable graph is restored from an
# already-built dump now (h-bootstrap/lion-prepare/ does the heavy,
# memory-hungry topology-building step once, outside this container) - kept
# generous anyway, since too low a limit does not slow a step down, the
# kernel kills it outright and the run ends with "killed by SIGKILL". Raise
# this if that happens; it is transient, and the services are still in
# standby while it runs, so the memory is there to lend.
BOOTSTRAP_MEM=48g
BOOTSTRAP_CPUS=6

SEED_DRIVERS=800
SEED_PASSENGERS=5000
HISTORY_DAYS=7
HISTORY_TRIPS_PER_DAY=2000
HISTORY_POSITIONS_PER_TRIP=8
# Historical trips are routed for real, through the same pgRouting query
# dispatch-service uses live. This many route lookups run at once.
HISTORY_ROUTING_WORKERS=8
# How far nus_common.routing.nearest_road_point() will snap a candidate
# point to the road network before giving up and calling it off the map
# (open water, a park, a real island with no car bridge). Shared by
# h-bootstrap, driver-service and passenger-service - every place a
# pickup, dropoff or driver location gets picked.
MAX_SNAP_KM=0.5

# The street map: NYC's own official LION data (DCP), not OpenStreetMap -
# real, DOT-verified one-way directions and posted speed limits, filtered to
# real drivable streets before anything else touches it. `make lion-fetch`
# downloads it once; `make lion-prepare` builds the routable graph from it
# once (both skip themselves when already done). Both are part of
# `make prepare`, so a normal deploy never thinks about either directly.
LION_URL=https://data.cityofnewyork.us/download/2v4z-66xt/application/zip
# The city's zones: NYC TLC's real, official Taxi Zones - the same 263
# neighborhood-shaped polygons Uber/Lyft/yellow cabs actually report trips
# against, not a synthetic rectangle grid. `make tlc-zones-fetch` downloads
# it once (part of `make prepare`, alongside lion-fetch); `make lion-prepare`
# builds city_zones_source from it in the same throwaway pass as the street
# graph.
TLC_ZONES_URL=https://data.cityofnewyork.us/api/geospatial/8meu-9t5y?method=export&format=GeoJSON
# One real month of TLC's High-Volume For-Hire Vehicle trip records, used to
# calibrate demand generation against real ride patterns instead of a flat
# random distribution - real busy zones/hours, real OD pairs, a real check
# on PLATFORM_COMMISSION_PCT. Pinned, not "the latest available": the same
# month always produces the same calibration, same reason RANDOM_SEED is
# pinned everywhere else. A few hundred MB, real billed traffic on a
# metered host - `make tlc-trips-fetch` downloads it once, on the host
# (part of `make prepare`, alongside lion-fetch/tlc-zones-fetch), and every
# zone-demand-prepare run after that reads the cached file instead of
# refetching it, including a retry after a failed run.
TLC_TRIP_DATA_MONTH=2025-01
TLC_TRIP_DATA_URL_TEMPLATE=https://d37ci6vzurychx.cloudfront.net/trip-data/fhvhv_tripdata_{month}.parquet
# The throwaway database `make lion-prepare` uses to build the graph. It
# exists for a few minutes on a private, stack-external network and is
# destroyed with the container - not a credential worth treating as one.
LION_PG_PASSWORD=throwaway-local-only
LION_PG_IMAGE=postgis/postgis:17-3.5
# How hard lion-pg is allowed to work while building the graph - tuned for
# speed, not durability, since the database is destroyed at the end of the
# same run. Sized for Dionysus (24 threads, 128GB RAM), this project's
# actual deployment target - override down for a smaller dev machine.
LION_PG_SHARED_BUFFERS=8GB
LION_PG_WORK_MEM=1GB
LION_PG_MAINTENANCE_WORK_MEM=4GB
LION_PG_MAX_WAL_SIZE=16GB
LION_PG_MAX_WORKER_PROCESSES=24
LION_PG_MAX_PARALLEL_WORKERS=16
LION_PG_MAX_PARALLEL_WORKERS_PER_GATHER=8
LION_PG_MAX_PARALLEL_MAINTENANCE_WORKERS=8
# How much of the host lion-pg/lion-prepare may use. Nothing else runs
# during this step (make prepare runs it before the real stack exists),
# so lion-pg gets the large share; lion-prepare is just a psql/ogr2ogr
# client waiting on it.
LION_PG_CPUS=20
LION_PREPARE_CPUS=4
# Leave false. True skips restoring the graph, and routing does not work without it.
SKIP_MAP_IMPORT=false
# Run the seeding steps again on a database that already has data. Existing
# rows are still kept; this only lifts the "already prepared" short-circuit.
FORCE_RESEED=false


# =============================================================================
# 14. PRICES
# =============================================================================
# Read by bootstrap AND dispatch, so live trips are priced the way the seeded
# week was. Changing one without the other makes the history disagree with
# the present — which is why they are one setting here.
FARE_BASE=3.0
FARE_PER_KM=1.75
FARE_PER_MINUTE=0.45


# =============================================================================
# 15. PACING — the volume dials
# =============================================================================
# These decide how many messages a second the stack has to carry. If it
# struggles, turn these down; never remove containers. DRIVER_TICK_SECONDS
# and TRIP_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE are the two that matter most.

# j-service-driver — a position per online driver per tick.
DRIVER_TICK_SECONDS=3.0
DRIVER_SPEED_KMH=25.0
DRIVER_ONLINE_SHARE=0.6
DRIVER_SHIFT_CHANGE_CHANCE=0.01
DRIVER_DB_SYNC_SECONDS=30.0
DRIVER_HOTSPOT_REFRESH_SECONDS=60.0

# k-service-passenger — every request becomes a routing query in dispatch,
# which is the most expensive step anywhere in the pipeline.
PASSENGER_TICK_SECONDS=5.0
TRIP_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=40.0

# l-service-dispatch
DISPATCH_TICK_SECONDS=1.0
DISPATCH_SEARCH_RADIUS_KM=5.0
DISPATCH_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_TICK=50
CANCEL_BY_DRIVER_CHANCE=0.06
CANCEL_BY_PASSENGER_CHANCE=0.07
TRIP_ACTIVE_TTL_SECONDS=7200

# m-service-city
CITY_SCORE_SECONDS=30.0
CITY_TRAFFIC_UPDATE_MINUTES=5.0
# Six hours: the length of one part of the day, so a score cannot outlive
# the period it describes.
HOTSPOT_TTL_SECONDS=21600
# Read by city-service AND the sink, so "is this a hotspot" means the same
# thing to the one that writes the score and the one that files it.
HOTSPOT_SCORE_THRESHOLD=0.6

# n-service-clickhouse-sink — the time limit is what keeps dashboards close
# to live when the city is quiet and a batch would take minutes to fill.
SINK_BATCH_ROWS=5000
SINK_FLUSH_SECONDS=5.0
SINK_HOTSPOT_REFRESH_SECONDS=30.0

# i-service-cache-updater
CDC_TOPIC_PATTERN=^cdc\..*
CACHE_BATCH_SIZE=500
CACHE_FLUSH_SECONDS=2.0

# Same seed in all three generators, so a run can be repeated exactly.
RANDOM_SEED=20250824


# =============================================================================
# 16. KAFKA CONSUMER GROUPS
# =============================================================================
# One per service, and they MUST stay distinct. Two services sharing a group
# do not each get a copy of the stream — Kafka splits the partitions between
# them, so each silently receives a fraction of what it expects and drops the
# rest. That is why these are six separate names rather than one setting.
#
# Renaming one makes that service re-read from its topics' start.
CACHE_UPDATER_GROUP_ID=cache-updater
DRIVER_GROUP_ID=driver-service
PASSENGER_GROUP_ID=passenger-service
DISPATCH_GROUP_ID=dispatch-service
CITY_GROUP_ID=city-service
SINK_GROUP_ID=clickhouse-sink

# =============================================================================
# 17. RESOURCE LIMITS — cpus / memory per container
# =============================================================================
# Every one of these defaults to exactly what shipped before it was tunable
# (root docker-compose.yaml's own preamble: a 20-core / 120 GB host, zero
# swap). Nothing here needs to change to deploy this stack — raise a value
# only if `make stats` shows a container actually pressed against its
# ceiling. h-bootstrap's own BOOTSTRAP_CPUS/BOOTSTRAP_MEM (section 13) work
# the same way and are the oldest example of this pattern.

# --- a-infra-postgres --------------------------------------------------------
ETCD_CPUS=0.15
ETCD_MEM=384m
# Historical trips are routed for real during bootstrap (h-bootstrap,
# section 13's HISTORY_ROUTING_WORKERS) - a burst of CPU-heavy pgRouting
# queries against whichever pg node is serving them. Confirmed against a real
# run: the replicas serving that burst pushed past 500% CPU on a 24-core,
# otherwise-idle host, well beyond a steady-state ceiling this low.
#
# PG_CPUS is the steady-state value pg-1/2/3 run at the rest of the time -
# every pg-* container is under 1% CPU outside the routing burst, so this
# stays low on purpose. PG_CPUS_BOOTSTRAP is only used by
# `make pg-cpus-bootstrap` / `make pg-cpus-normal`, which h-bootstrap's own
# `make bootstrap` calls automatically around the run: raise it before,
# lower it back after a successful run (left raised after a failed one, so a
# retry does not re-pay the ramp-up cost). Each recreates pg-1/2/3, since a
# running container's cpus: cannot change without one.
PG_CPUS=2
PG_CPUS_BOOTSTRAP=10
PG_MEM=9g
ETCD_CERTGEN_CPUS=0.25
ETCD_CERTGEN_MEM=64m

# --- b-infra-redis ------------------------------------------------------------
REDIS_CPUS=0.5
REDIS_MEM=4g
SENTINEL_CPUS=0.1
SENTINEL_MEM=192m

# --- c-infra-kafka --------------------------------------------------------------
KAFKA_CPUS=1.2
KAFKA_MEM=5.5g
KAFKA_DIRS_CPUS=0.25
KAFKA_DIRS_MEM=64m
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_CPUS=0.2
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_MEM=1g
KSQLDB_SECRETS_CPUS=0.25
KSQLDB_SECRETS_MEM=64m
KSQLDB_CPUS=0.5
KSQLDB_MEM=1.5g
KAFKA_TOPICS_CPUS=0.25
KAFKA_TOPICS_MEM=512m

# --- d-infra-debezium ------------------------------------------------------------
DEBEZIUM_CPUS=0.4
DEBEZIUM_MEM=2g
CONNECTOR_REGISTER_CPUS=0.25
CONNECTOR_REGISTER_MEM=256m

# --- root (one-shots and the entry tier) ------------------------------------
VOLUME_PERMS_CPUS=0.25
VOLUME_PERMS_MEM=64m
HAPROXY_RENDER_CPUS=0.25
HAPROXY_RENDER_MEM=64m
# Shared by both lb-a and lb-b.
LB_CPUS=0.1
LB_MEM=128m

# --- e-infra-clickhouse -----------------------------------------------------
CH_KEEPER_CPUS=0.1
CH_KEEPER_MEM=1g
CH_CPUS=1.3
CH_MEM=8g
CH_SECRETS_CPUS=0.25
CH_SECRETS_MEM=64m
CH_DDL_CPUS=0.25
CH_DDL_MEM=512m

# --- f-infra-grafana / g-infra-superset --------------------------------------
GRAFANA_CPUS=0.2
GRAFANA_MEM=1g
SUPERSET_CPUS=0.6
SUPERSET_MEM=3g
SUPERSET_INIT_CPUS=0.5
SUPERSET_INIT_MEM=2g

# --- i through n — the six live services -------------------------------------
CACHE_UPDATER_CPUS=0.15
CACHE_UPDATER_MEM=768m
DRIVER_SERVICE_CPUS=0.15
DRIVER_SERVICE_MEM=768m
PASSENGER_SERVICE_CPUS=0.15
PASSENGER_SERVICE_MEM=768m
DISPATCH_SERVICE_CPUS=0.15
DISPATCH_SERVICE_MEM=768m
CITY_SERVICE_CPUS=0.15
CITY_SERVICE_MEM=768m
CLICKHOUSE_SINK_CPUS=0.15
CLICKHOUSE_SINK_MEM=768m
